# Teaching methodology

- Track A: explain every prerequisite concept before depending on it; no unexplained jargon.
- Track B: major chapters include a **Revision and Mastery** section with common gaps and advanced questions.
- Use simple examples first, then realistic engineering scenarios; explain important lines of code.
- When a related idea first appears (JVM, GC, thread, generics, DI, etc.), give a minimum viable explanation immediately, even if a later chapter goes deeper.
- Separate exercises from solutions; do not spoil practice with immediate answers.
- Core Java and foundations before Spring Boot and heavy framework material.
- When teaching frameworks, explain underlying Java mechanisms (interfaces, reflection, proxies, etc.).
- Preserve dual-audience value: accessible for beginners, technically meaningful for experienced developers.
